INSERT INTO `quest_offer_reward` (`ID`, `Emote1`, `Emote2`, `Emote3`, `Emote4`, `EmoteDelay1`, `EmoteDelay2`, `EmoteDelay3`, `EmoteDelay4`, `RewardText`, `VerifiedBuild`) VALUES
(175000, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Sent by one of my knights, you say?$B$BVery well. In these matters, I trust their judgment more than my own. I accept your services as... a squire? A mercenary?$B$B<Servin Darengar waves a hand, as if dismissing the matter of titles.>$B$BForgive me, I find it difficult to think clearly. I\'ve been shut in here for hours, trying to convince the House of Nobles to send the army to Scadeald... with little success.', 0),
(175001, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<You can see defeat written clearly in the old man\'s watery eyes.>$B$BIt would have taken a miracle to convince this lot of cowards to take the initiative for once. You heard it yourself: as long as Lady Serenya Coldmere, last of her name, refuses to formally request aid, which she never will, the House of Nobles will not intervene.$B$BThe blood of my people will stain their hands, but not mine. I will not sit idly by while Scadeald withers away.', 0),
(175002, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The handmaid clenches her fists against her apron, her knuckles white.>$B$BAre you here for my lord Arathon?$B$BYou are too late, then. Those brutes from Graysky, sent by that magpie Lady Serenya, arrested him hours ago. They wouldn\'t even let him put on his boots.$B$BAnd now they are turning the manor upside down! Can you believe it? They have accused the Darengar family of high treason!', 0),
(175003, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'This is...$B$B<As she flips through the pages of the journal, Cadmilla\'s expression shifts from sorrow to confusion.>$B$BI don\'t understand any of this.$B$B<Cadmilla shakes her head.>$B$BIt is Lord Arathon\'s handwriting, there is no doubt of that. Oh, oh dear! How could he...?', 0),
(175004, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Splintered crates bearing the seal of the Alliance. It appears they were tossed from the top of the well with criminal haste. The fall shattered several, scattering Stormwind steel across the damp floor of the cave.>$B$B<This isn\'t refuse. It\'s an arsenal.>', 0),
(175005, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The captain beckons you from a distance with a grim gesture.>$B$BThe handmaid has told me everything.$B$BWe had grave suspicions that the Darengars were conspiring against the government of Graysky, but to think they would dare go this far...$B$BThey were arming the Ealdir! I imagine their intent was to deepen the crisis facing Scadeald, further undermining Lady Serenya\'s rule.', 0),
(175006, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Even through the veil obscuring Serenya\'s face, you feel the coldness of her eyes, piercing through you like twin arrows.>$B$BA turn of events worthy of House Darengar. I shall personally see to it that history remembers their name as a synonym for betrayal.$B$BAs for your involvement, you have the gratitude of Scadeald. Someone of your caliber could be of great service to this province.', 0),
(175007, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You come recommended by Lady Serenya, do you?$B$B<Draewyn strikes you as one of those men who need not raise their voice to command respect. His gray eyes size you up from head to toe.>$B$BScadeald is generous in two things: rain and trouble. The Light knows we need every soul we can muster. Very well! You look competent. Diligent. That\'s already more than I can say for most here!', 0),
(175009, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'So, they finally deigned to send someone! Better late than never, I suppose; even if you are just a sellsword.$B$BForgive me for taking my frustration out on you. It\'s been a fortnight since I dispatched my report to Sir Draewyn, and I\'ve had naught but silence from Graysky ever since.$B$BI know the guard is stretched thin with the Ealdir and the Frostmane trolls, but something has happened in this village that should worry us far more than a roadside ambush.', 0),
(175010, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'A new recruit?$B$BYou must be something exceptional, if the Lord Commander believes one more pair of hands will tip the scales in our favor.$B$BWhat do you know of the Frostmane trolls? Do you have any idea what you\'re stepping into? Cutting down bandits or dealing with savages in loincloths is one thing. The battlefield is another entirely.$B$BAnd the Frostmane trolls are implacable foes.', 0),
(175011, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Sent by Sir Draewyn, are you?$B$BGood, good. I know things are not going well in Graysky. The troll threat, the Kingsway being cut off...$B$B<The priest heaves a weary, nervous sigh.>$B$BThe crypts. I wrote to the Lord Commander regarding the crypts, but it would be best if you saw them with your own eyes.', 0),
(175012, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'So, the chaplain from Graysky sends you. What was that man\'s name? Centhis? Contis? Cuntir? Bah!$B$BTruth be told, the Graysky Guard would do little more than get in the way here at the abbey. We are better off as we are.$B$BBut since you are here, I can think of a thing or two you might help with.', 0),
(175013, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The Light is present here, in the sermons and the prayers. But it was also present in the screams, the blood, and the tears of the initiates you challenged.$B$BYou have done them a favor. \'Spare the rod, spoil the child,\' my father used to say. He used to say many things, mostly nonsense, but in this... In this, he was right.', 0),
(175014, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Father Amrod sent...? Agh!$B$B<Diallos has lost too much blood. He seems barely conscious of his surroundings.>$B$BThese troggs are not like the others. They ambushed us. A trap. All of it.$B$BI am the only one left alive...', 0),
(175015, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The trogg filth is history. Good.$B$BDo not let their deaths weigh on you any more than the deaths of the crusaders I sent to deal with them weigh on my conscience.$B$BOn one hand, troggs lack souls. And on the other, the crusaders swore to sacrifice their lives in service to the Light.', 0),
(175016, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'What have we here?$B$B<Enharon inspects the artifact with a gaze that is equal parts curiosity and repulsion.>$B$BThose abominations! I never imagined they could possess a religion of their own; that they would have sufficient intelligence. This is detestable. A faith bound to the radiation of Gnomeregan, no less.$B$BIt cannot be a coincidence. Nothing survived Gnomeregan unchanged. If my suspicions are correct, then the radiation mutated these troggs: it granted them a certain degree of intellect.$B$BKilling them was an indulgence.', 0),
(175017, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'A king? <Father Amrod laughs heartily, a loud, mocking roar.>$B$BThose animals. They don\'t even deserve the air they breathe. A king. A king! What will be next? Princesses? Troubadours? Priests?$B$B<His tone changes, hardening. His countenance turns dark.>$B$BDeath is the only remedy that works on those beasts.', 0),
(175018, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Oh, thank you, thank you so much! You\'ve recovered more than I dared hope for; enough to save me from ruin, at least.$B$BI\'ll try to repair the wagon, and as soon as Tito recovers, I\'ll resume my journey toward Graysky.$B$BI don\'t know how I can ever pay you back, but I am forever in your debt.', 0),
(175019, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You return stained in red. Good.$B$BThese woods are a little safer thanks to you. The Stormwind Guard is committed to keeping this area protected; even so, proceed with caution if you plan to continue your journey toward Scadeald.$B$BReports have reached us from the Graysky Guard, warning about the Ealdir. It seems their attacks are becoming increasingly frequent.', 0),
(175020, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'A bite! I\'m sure it\'s thanks to you.$B$BThank you very much for your help. I don\'t have much to offer, but please accept this as a token of my gratitude.', 0),
(175021, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The fisherman takes the plants and begins to rub them between his hands. Once they reach a paste-like consistency, he applies it directly to his wounds.>$B$BAgggh! You\'ve no idea how it stings... but if it nips, it heals...$B$BThank you so much. I can already feel the sensation returning. I think I can manage on my own from here, but before you go, take this in thanks for your help.', 0),
(175022, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You... you did it!? Woooaaah!$B$BI wish I could be as brave as you when I grow up. Thank you so much!$B$BHere, keep this. In the sewers... I won\'t be needing it.', 0),
(175023, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Despite appearances, the creature before you retains a vestige of humanity in its eyes that makes it difficult to perceive it entirely as a monster.>$B$BWait! Wait. I. I not like others. Still think. Speak. We speak? Yes?$B$BMiners. Mining. Cave. Yes? Cave-in! Rocks and dead. Miners trapped. Hunger. Bad hunger. We eat dead. Then... then claws grow. Hair grow. Teeth grow.$B$BTurn into monsters! Many lose mind. Others no. Others like me. Others scared.', NULL),
(175024, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'A new face, and you aren\'t carrying pitchforks and torches, for a change. Not a bad start. Tell me, what brings you to me?$B$B<You recount the story of Alto Bilberg and the curse the miners seem to have suffered, transforming them into gnolls.>$B$BTypical. They fear and despise you until the day they suddenly need you. But for all these years, I have lived in \'otherness,\' condemned to the contempt and constant suspicion of the people of Dunshire.$B$BWhy should I help them?', 0),
(175026, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'It didn\'t work, did it? I can see it on your face.$B$B<Beldred twists her face into a grimace of disgust. It seems to be a genuine sentiment.>$B$BI\'m afraid there is nothing more I can do, then. I would consult my coven, but they are far away and busy fighting a pointless war against Graysky. And in any case, the price they would demand for their help is far beyond your means.$B$BAlthough...', 0),
(175027, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Amaranthe\'s gaze carries a certain sense of nakedness. Her eyes are two wells of deep memory.>$B$BA soldier\'s burden is a heart heavier than their sword. I know what brings you before me. Your effort is noble, and your intentions pure.$B$BBut I cannot help you.$B$BThe Gilded Vale endures dark days, and my power wanes alongside the vitality of the forest.$B$BI\'m afraid I cannot be of assistance. Not until we thwart the threat that looms over us all.', NULL),
(175028, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The wind isn\'t blowing today... You\'ll have to wait until we finish grinding what we already have.$B$BThen you can grind your wheat.', 0),
(175029, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Look at that! You\'ve brought plenty of flour, fantastic.$B$BThat\'s one less ingredient to worry about for the famous Dunshire Pie.', 0),
(175030, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'We have everything!$B$BDo you want me to start preparing it now, or has your hunger not kicked in yet, you sweet tooth?$B$BBah, what a question... as soon as the smell hits you, your mouth will be watering!', 0),
(175031, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Right now, you are the envy of all Scadeald. Seriously; do you have any idea how many people would love to be in your shoes? Tasting the Dunshire Pie for the very first time...$B$BSavor the moment. Eat without haste. Fill your belly. You never forget that first bite!', 0),
(175032, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The amulet, you found it!$B$BYou have no idea what a tight spot you\'ve gotten me out of. Now I can focus on preparing the shipment. And I\'ll be able to get some sleep at night, for a change.$B$BI\'ll let you know when everything is ready, if you\'re willing. I believe you\'ve earned the right to see the Gilded Vale with your own eyes.', 0),
(175033, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'A new face. Hm. Judging by the pack upon your back, I take it you carry my order. Darond sent you, then.$B$BWell, friend, take a breath and set your mind at ease. You will find no evil in this Sanctuary that you did not bring with you.', NULL),
(175034, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The people of Cresthairn thank you.$B$BThe death of these bears will bring peace to the region, and their furs will provide warmth when the winds of Dun Morogh blow.', 0),
(175035, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Stop. Get down, or you\'ll get us both killed. Up ahead are the Ealdmaine caverns, an Ealdir den if ever there was one.$B$BIn Graysky, they were offering a handsome sum for the head of the Ealdmaine leader. My mouth started watering at the thought.$B$B<Randec clenches his teeth to stifle a groan of pain. An arrow is buried in his knee.>$B$BDammit.', 0),
(175036, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You.$B$B<Raigh Anramor stops you, placing a hand on your shoulder. Immediately after, he brings his nose close to the stolen furs of your disguise. His face wrinkles into a grimace.>$B$BYou reek of death. But I do not know your face. The Ealdmaine caverns are not the pathetic little village where you were born. Real Ealdir come here. Those with the hearts of warriors. To train. To kill.', 0),
(175039, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You\'ve done well. More than well. If all the Cresthairn militiamen were like you, this civil war would have ended years ago.$B$BYou\'ve chosen the right side, friend. Not like my brother. I tried to make him see reason, believe me. But there was no way.$B$BI did what I had to do. Yes, sir. I did the only sensible thing.', 0),
(175040, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Aralia\'s face is the very image of pain, devastation, and ruin. Something breaks inside her, behind her kind eyes. Something that will never heal. Never. Not in this life, nor in the next, nor in any other.>$B$BI... Why? Why me? Why my sons?$B$BAbellon. Oh, Abellon. Dead. Dead at the hands of his own brother.$B$BWhat did I do wrong?', 0),
(175042, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Have you dealt with the undead?$B$BThank the Light! And thanks to the Lord Commander for sending you! And to you, of course, for doing the deed.$B$BI still do not understand how this could have happened. What manner of spell must those witches have conjured to profane the sanctity of those graves and raise their guests as undead?', 0),
(175043, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The creature turns an afflicted, pleading gaze upon you.>$B$BYou... must... help us...$B$BMy husband... he did something...$B$BThe Light... Tainted...$B$BWe... cannot... die...', 0),
(175046, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Fine stock you\'ve gathered! Even more than I expected!$B$BA promise is a promise, so here you go. And hey... if you ever want to work for a humble shopkeeper, come back around.', 0),
(175047, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Dead dogs don\'t bite... or so we say. Though I\'m not sure that old proverb applies to these bastards. Sooner or later, more Ealdir will show up to occupy the cave.$B$BThey always do.$B$BOne way or another, you have the gratitude of the province of Scadeald. This stretch of the road will be safer for a while thanks to you.', 0),
(175048, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The creatures... the rifts...$B$BHow did you...?$B$B<Holga loses consciousness as she whispers that final question.>', 0),
(175051, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Well, well! The Sergeant must be feeling generous; it\'s been a couple of days since he last sent us any reinforcements.$B$BLet me get a proper look at you. <The lieutenant inspects you with an amused, mocking gaze. Finally, he gives you a friendly slap on the shoulder.>$B$BAnd it looks like you lopped off a few heads on your way here. Good work, indeed. Good work.$B$BKeep that attitude up!', 0),
(175053, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The hostages are safe.$B$BThose in better condition have decided to risk the trek through the forest toward Graysky. The rest have taken refuge in the old watchtower.$B$BGood work, $C.', 0),
(175054, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Frieren looks at the sack with suspicion. Upon checking its contents, the expression on his face shifts from disgust to agitation.>$B$BAn orc? Here?$B$B<For a fleeting moment, the sack seems as if it might slip through his fingers.>$B$BThen the Horde is allied with the Frostmane trolls. The ambush, the war... none of it was by chance.$BWe must inform Graysky. But first, I want you to see to one last thing.', 0),
(175056, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Your spirit has brought warmth to this watchtower.$B$BStay as long as you need by the fire.', 0),
(175057, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The troll. The beast... Are they dead?$B$B<The soldier closes his eyes and, for the first time in a long while, he feels something akin to \'peace\'.>$B$BNo one can return my fallen comrades to me. Not even the Light. But you have given me something that comes close.$B$BI have little with which to reward you, but I wish to do so. Please, accept this.', 0),
(175059, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The Lord Commander\'s face is a mask that betrays no emotion. But his eyes... Oh, his eyes prophesy war. With a certainty that is terrifying.>$B$BThe Horde.$B$BEverything is simpler, then. And more complicated at the same time.$B$B<An eternal silence follows. Then, he adds:>$B$BYou have exceeded my expectations. That does not often happen. I shall see to it that you are rewarded accordingly. For now, as long as I remain Lord Commander, consider this castle your home.', 0),
(175060, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The Lieutenant takes a deep breath; as he notices the acrid scent of burning flesh in the air, he narrows his eyes.>$B$BI know it isn\'t easy... Doing the right thing rarely is.', 0),
(175062, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'What is this?$B$B<As he inspects the parchment, Alcarian\'s eyebrows shift from a flat line to a sharp diagonal. When he finally looks up, his eyes betray sheer panic.>$B$BAnd you say you\'ve thwarted it? You killed the druids?$B$B<A sigh of relief. He presses a hand to his chest.>$B$BGood. Phew. Yes. You did well coming to me. Yes. Yes! I shall organize an investigation! That\'s it! I promise to get to the root of this matter.', 0),
(175063, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Another camp liquidated.$B$B<Draewyn\'s breathing is heavy. His breath, sharp and ragged.>$B$BAt least none of our own had to die this time.$B$BThank you for your service.', 0),
(175067, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Dorothy...? Oh...$B$B<Upon seeing the bundle of food, you watch as the hope dies in her eyes.>$B$BFernad sent you. I... Thank you, I suppose. But I have no appetite. I cannot sit idly by while my daughter is missing.$B$BI must find her!', NULL),
(175068, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You killed them... didn\'t you?$B$BMaybe you should kill more. Until not a single murloc is left.$B$BDorothy didn\'t deserve this. I didn\'t deserve to lose her...$B$BThey took her from me. Make them give Dorothy back.$B$B<Ralda breaks into tears.>', NULL),
(175069, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'What do you mean you found nothing? But the murlocs... Dorothy...$B$B<Ralda\'s gaze darkens.>$B$BAre you hiding something from me? You wouldn\'t dare! Even if Dorothy weren\'t... If she... <She swallows hard. The lump in her throat remains.> I would rather know than live with the uncertainty.$B$BSo tell me. Because you must have found something.$B$BIs she still alive? Please!$B$B<Ralda breaks down into tears.>', NULL),
(175070, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Are they dead? Did they suffer? Did they scream in pain?$B$B<The hatred burns so intensely in Ralda\'s eyes that it almost seems as if her tears are evaporating.>$B$BNot enough, I\'m sure.', NULL),
(175071, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'What do you mean you found nothing? Are you sure? Not a single trace of Dorothy? Impossible... Impossible... Impossible!$B$BHer father punished her and took her to the mine with the kobolds. She has to be there!$B$BYou didn\'t look well enough... You have to go back and search harder. She has to be there... somewhere.', NULL),
(175072, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Take this, small as it may be, as compensation for your trouble.$B$BThank you for caring for my daughter, may the Light keep her, and for my poor wife.', NULL),
(175073, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'From Graysky, you say? That old skinflint Dolcrest... if it were up to him, he\'d hoard every last one of my cheeses.$B$BTell him not to worry: I\'ll have his order ready in two shakes of a lamb\'s tail.$B$BTo be honest, I owe much of my reputation to the people of Graysky. The southern provinces do nothing but hinder exports due to pressure from certain nobles.$B$BYou know what? It\'s their loss. Our cheeses are the finest in the Kingdom of Stormwind.', NULL),
(175074, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Look at that color! And it smells wonderful! Smooth as silk, creamy, sweet, and with a fresh aroma that brings to mind the green meadows of Windswept...$B$BNothing, absolutely nothing, compares to the milk from our cows.', NULL),
(175076, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You\'ve left \'em lookin\' right handsome! And lean, too, yes $gsir:ma\'am;. Very well done. If only I could shed a few pounds that easily...$B$BMany thanks for the help. Want to see just how good Windswept wool is? Take my hat. I\'ll just make myself a new one with all the wool you\'ve brought me.', NULL),
(175077, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Well done! Many thanks on behalf of Shadewell and Windswept. This wolf has caused us more headaches than any other.', NULL),
(175078, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'What are you, a mercenary? Fine by me; you could be a murloc in a Starcaller disguise for all I care. Fact is, I need people with grit, those who don\'t flinch easily.$B$BThis isn\'t some skirmish like the ones Graysky has been fighting these past few weeks. This is war. Real war. And if the Fortress of Andraste falls, the Blackrock orcs will spread through the province like a plague.', NULL),
(175079, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Are you with Arathel\'s lot? Hm. Good. You fought well down there.', NULL),
(175080, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The Blackrock catapults were becoming quite the problem, and my soldiers weren\'t advancing through their ranks fast enough to reach them. You have rendered a fine service, $C.', NULL),
(175081, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Moore sent you? It\'s good to know the Commander still remembers me.$B$BIn my experience, I\'ll tell you this: battle tightens the stomach and loosens the bowels. But victory... victory is always followed by a ravenous hunger. And I cannot provide meat for an entire stronghold on my own.$B$BIf you want to be of use, prepare to get your hands dirty.', NULL),
(175083, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'A friendly face. We welcome few of those, lately. It seems as though the world has filled with shadows.$B$BCome and tell us: what brings you here?$B$B<The elf\'s gaze strips away your intentions. He nods, as if understanding.>$B$BAh, the hunter sent you. I know what they say of us in Andraste! Ask not the elves for advice, because they will tell you both \'yes\' and \'no\'.$B$B<He laughs.>$B$BBut advice is a dangerous gift, even from the wise to the wise, and all courses may run ill.$B$BStill, we shall endeavor to help you.', NULL),
(175084, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'I wouldn\'t believe it if I hadn\'t seen it with my own eyes. Not even ten paladins could have done for these men what the enchanted water from that amphora has achieved.$B$BI knew the elves of the valley were powerful allies, but I always preferred to keep them out of this war, given their reserved nature.$B$BPerhaps I should reconsider.', NULL),
(175085, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'More than one of them shed tears. Poor devils.$B$BIt\'s a cruel world, ours. But it would be far crueler to send them to the battlefield before tearing the blindfolds from their eyes.', NULL),
(175088, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Samara takes the documents with an anxious gesture. She does not meet your gaze again until she has finished reading them. Her expression is grim.>$B$BI welcome the news of Kurgan\'s death. But this is not over. Not by a long shot.', NULL),
(175089, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Good. That will slow them down. Long enough, at least, for our warning to reach Northshire in time.$B$BSo much violence... <Samara rubs her temples with a weary gesture.> But it serves a purpose:$B$BWe fight, we bleed, and we sweat so that others do not have to know the horrors of war. So that the people of Stormwind may live in peace.', NULL),
(175090, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Despite the shouts and orders he barks every few seconds, Commander Moore listens to your report attentively.>$B$BSo they were headed for Northshire.$B$BI see.$B$BYou have bought us time, $N. Enough to avert a tragedy.$B$BYou have the gratitude of this fortress.', NULL),
(175091, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You\'re... you\'re not an orc, are you?$B$B<Darvin lets out a thunderous sigh, as if he\'d been holding his breath for hours.>$B$BBy the beards of my ancestors! I\'ve never been so terrified! When the stones started flying and those war horns sounded... I thought for sure they\'d find me.', NULL),
(175092, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'What have we here?$B$B<Darvin\'s eyes scan the Dark Iron missives, his brow furrowing with growing concern.>$B$BThe Guild. They call themselves the Shadowburn Guild. So, they aren\'t your typical Dark Iron, then.$B$BAnd this...<The dwarf\'s gaze darkens.>$B$BThis is troubling.', NULL),
(175093, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Barin listens to your account with either patience or indifference. It is impossible to tell.>"$B$BAndraste, is it? That is... south, yes?$B$BAs I see it, they are not our problem. A Dark Iron cult aspiring to liberate their clan from the Firelord\'s yoke? It reeks of civil war.$B$BI say let them kill each other.', 0),
(175094, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Thank you. It\'s not as bad as it looks, I think. It was barely a scratch, but it hurts as if a dagger had been driven into me.$b$bMay the Light bless Erendel for sending you to find me. And bless you as well, for lending your aid.$b$bThese sprites... Ow!', 0),
(175095, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'This should be more than enough. I thank you, friend.$b$bDo not worry about me. I will prepare a poultice with the Belorite you\'ve brought, though it will take me a while. In any case, I am no longer in danger.$b$bGo on your way, please. Do not linger here uselessly with me. You have already helped me more than I can ever thank you for.', 0),
(175096, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'So, you come from the Sanctuary. Lady Amaranthe\'s intuition is correct: the forest is growing restless.$b$bThe Blood Elves are the reason. Their irresponsible consumption of the valley\'s magic disturbs the balance. They have brought those vile crystals with them and are using them to drain the very life from the woods.$b$bAnd the forest rebels.$b$bThe danger is greater than you suspect. For this forest is not just a forest.', 0),
(175098, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'All life is precious, and every Dryad you have saved counts.$b$bYou have my immortal gratitude, $r.', 0),
(175099, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Cauranos\'s warning comes too late, I fear. Moments ago, the Blood Elves succeeded in shattering the protective sigils that sealed the Chambers of Erdaya.$B$BI have sent my finest warriors, the elite of the Sanctuary. But they will not be enough.$B$BWe need you.', NULL),
(175100, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'It was a trap. They killed everyone... How could so many have infiltrated the diplomatic retinue? I should have realized. I should have...!$B$B<A coughing fit interrupts the Dark Ranger. The wound on her chest bleeds a black liquid that looks very foul indeed.>$B$BThey are a cult. They call themselves The Family, I believe. I tried to stop them, but I nearly suffered the same fate as the warriors sent by your Sanctuary\'s lady. The same fate as all the others...', NULL),
(175101, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'I heard them scream. I heard them scream...', NULL),
(175102, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'So, it was a cult. A pity. This will complicate our relations with the Blood Elves. And certainly, it will rouse the suspicion of both the Horde and the Alliance.$B$BNen\'alah has never seen such violence. And we have never had to mourn so many of our own.$B$BBut the danger has passed, and the valley breathes. In time, the forest will heal; we will heal. And the Namarien Sanctuary will hear laughter and songs once more.', NULL),
(175103, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You. You here!$B$BWhat be this?$B$B<Alto Bilberg sniffs the seed. Though the scent seems to repel him at first, his pupils dilate.>$B$BWe plant? This heal? Not witch magic. Different magic. Stronger.$B$BFlower... Me like flowers.', NULL),
(175106, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Are they all...? You know. <Daendor makes a gesture with his thumb across his neck. Then he makes a funny sound and sticks his tongue out to the side.>$B$BGood, good. Yes. Ah...$B$B<A sudden sadness washes over him. His shoulders slump.>$B$BNo, no. I\'m fine. It\'s just that... now that you\'ve dealt with the Ealdir, I have no reason to stay. And I don\'t want to leave Namarien Sanctuary. I don\'t want to go back to Graysky.', NULL),
(175107, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You\'re Sir Draewyn\'s trusted man, aren\'t you?$b$bWelcome to Rockrend, I suppose. I visited this village more than once before misfortune befell it. They always seemed like homebody folk to me.', 0),
(175109, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The woman cuts a haggard figure: her hair is a tangled mess, her clothes tattered and torn; her feet are blackened and bloodied. She almost looks like an Ealdir herself.>$b$bHave you finished off those monsters? Then we should fear you even more than them. Are you... you are on our side, right?', 0),
(175110, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'As I suspected, then. How many did you say were hiding in the mine?$b$b<Thandel narrows his eyes and shakes his head slowly, his brow furrowed.>$b$bSo few. Far too few. This village will never recover from such a blow, even if we manage to save every soul they abducted.', 0),
(175111, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Ah, a friendly face! Light knows, one more day with Telperion and I\'d have strangled him, if only to get him to relax his face for once; he\'s been scowling all day.$B$BTell me, what\'s your name? The short one sent you, didn\'t he? Have you turned up anything in Rockrend?$B$BI\'m afraid we haven\'t made much headway. We\'ve no idea where the captives were taken, nor why.', 0),
(175114, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You killed him? By the Light, you actually killed him!$B$B<Laurelin embraces you enthusiastically.>$B$BWith him gone, the Ealdir of Braicag Heights will no longer pose a threat to our men. You know what they say: dead dogs...$B$BCan\'t bite.', 0),
(175115, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The runes of the Ealdir act as anchors. Through them, they channel the magic that flows naturally from their land. A dark magic, tainted.$B$BThe answer has eluded me for a long time, but I believe I shall gain more than one insight into the nature of this shadow by studying the runestones you have brought me.$B$BYou have my gratitude.', 0),
(175116, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The witch is dead. Yes, I felt it; like a burden becoming lighter, or a pain made easier to bear.$B$BNo more nightmares, then. No more night-terrors. No more paranoia.$B$BAt last, I can focus on what I truly came here to do.', 0),
(175117, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Deirdre\'s hands, tipped with impossibly long nails, scratch your flank and caress your brow.>$B$BGood cat, yes indeed. Such a fine little puss.$B$BWhat has brought you to my shack, hm? Let me guess! You want me to spill our plans? Our darkest, most delicious secrets. You\'d like that, wouldn\'t you, petal?$B$B<Deirdre cackles with cruel laughter.>$B$BFoolish $gboy:girl;. Did you think such a crude spell could deceive me? Come now, let\'s dispense with the theatrics. Let us speak face to face, you and I.', 0),
(175118, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The Rooted Shadow. Merely speaking its name aloud makes the very sunlight wither.$B$BI shall help you save those poor souls from the sacrificial altar. But, if we are to stop Morgwena, we too must be prepared to sacrifice, and to risk, everything.', 0),
(175119, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Word has spread of how we broke the curse weighing upon those Ealdir. They saw you leave my shack, so they believe it was our doing: my magic and your brawn.$B$BIf only they knew that shattering the masks was enough... But I like it better this way; let them fear us.', 0),
(175120, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Have you secured the Great Stag\'s blessing?$B$BYes. I can feel it radiating from you even from here.$B$BGood.', 0),
(175121, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'I\'m busy, in case you hadn\'t noticed! What the dev—?$B$B<The cook cuts himself short when you show him the vial of poison, his voice dropping to a whisper at once.>$B$BSo, they sent you. Fine then. Why not? Isn\'t it they say, third time\'s a charm? I was just finishing the bread. Lady Serenya does enjoy a glass of wine to wash it down.', 0),
(175123, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'My heart ceased its beating long ago. In all these years, I have never stopped imploring the Light for an answer:$B$B\'Why me?\'$B$BMy husband was the Lord of Scadeald, and the only way I could think of to honor him, the only way to give meaning to my grotesque existence, was by ruling this province. By protecting it.$B$BBut now I see with clarity what the Light\'s design for me truly was.$B$BThis is the only path left to take.', 0),
(175124, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'This is...$B$B<Deirdre inspects Lady Serenya\'s ring with something akin to greed. The ring is a masterpiece of craftsmanship unlike any you have seen; the sapphire set within it alone is worth more than all the Ealdir\'s jewels combined.>$B$BYou killed her, then. It won\'t be long before the word spreads.$B$BGood. Good! Now that Serenya is off the board, only Morgwena remains.', 0),
(175125, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Morgwena\'s grimoire! Centuries of knowledge fermented in rituals and spells of the darkest nature.$B$BThe particulars of the ritual she is conducting must be contained within these pages.$B$BI shall require time to study it in detail.', 0),
(175128, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'More survivors? I shall see to it that a company of my finest men is sent to the beach to bring them to safety at once!', 0),
(175129, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'I see you\'ve returned covered in blood.$B$B<The dwarf wrinkles his nose as the smell reaches him.>$B$BMurlocs? Though... it smells different. Ah, well. I suppose you gave them a proper thrashing. Good. Let them crawl back to the ocean whining. The farther they are from our shores, the better. We\'ve enough troubles to bear as it is.', 0),
(175139, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The alarms have gone silent! Ha!$B$BLet me calculate...$B$B<He murmurs, punctuating every word with a clack on his mechanical console.>$B$BThe tape has a durability of 7 over 3... the population has been reduced by a value of 15... the temperature is fluctuating by 6 to 7 degrees... and the Tram\'s flow has increased by \'2E\'...$B$B<He slams a key and the result is immediate. He shouts enthusiastically:>$B$BOne hundred and thirty-seven percent!', NULL),
(175140, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The Alarm-o-Bot approaches with a waddling, duck-like gait. Though its red light blinds you every time it flashes, you sense a distinct mechanical distress in its bolts.>$B$BWARNING! ACTION REQUIRED!$BWARNING! WARNING!', NULL),
(175144, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Naudin shakes his head as he connects a bundle of cables to the Alarm-o-Bot, stripping it for parts in a matter of seconds. As he pries out components and jacks circuits into the central mainframe, the Alarm-o-Bot lets out agonizing screeches and erratic flashes.>$B$BAn attack orchestrated by a Dark Iron sorcerer? With a trogg army at his beck and call!$B$BI don\'t know what to make of this. An assault of this nature requires preparation. Organization. Intent.$B$BWhat exactly did the Dark Irons hope to gain from all this?', NULL),
(175147, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Foul creatures... Even killing them turns my stomach. Their blood stains the snow. Their tracks defile our paths. Those abominable, two-toed feet!$B$BAt least, in death, they cease to breathe our air. Our mountain breeze.$B$BI\'d give you a proper embrace if I had any strength left in me!', 0),
(175148, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'They are few, but then, my hope was a fool\'s hope.$B$BWith luck, Bromli of Daumor Peak will be able to spare a few of his own gryphons once we\'ve reclaimed the Winter\'s Path.', 0),
(175149, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'That...$B$B<Dargran slowly lowers his rifle.>$B$BThat head has Erdruin\'s handiwork written all over it, I wager. So, that old curmudgeon is still kicking, is he? Now, why in the world would I want a severed troll head?$B$BRight macabre, the lot of it.$B$BStill, seeing as you\'re here, I could use an extra pair of hands.', 0),
(175150, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Good.$B$BWithout the Horde\'s backing, it\'ll be like sending them back to the Stone Age.', 0),
(175151, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'I saw it all from up here, aye.$B$BDoing what\'s right is rarely easy.', 0),
(175152, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Ah, my friend!$B$BThis tale would have ended quite differently, I suspect, had you not arrived.$B$BMy name is Thrymus the Astray. I am the Custodian of this place.$B$BI admit, it has seen better days...$B$BBut enough with the pleasantries. What has transpired here is without precedent in ten thousand years.', 0),
(175153, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You have courage, standing before me after slaughtering so many of my followers.$B$BStill, I find you amusing. And you may prove useful yet.$B$BYou are here because you seek answers, are you not?$B$BI shall give them to you.', 0),
(175154, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'This is my temple. Or so it was, once.$B$BThose aberrations slaughtered my priests and drove away the faithful.', 0),
(175155, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'What is worse: the Sicklight Children have never before ventured so far from their irradiated lands.$B$BI believe they saw the opportunity presented by the Horde\'s campaign, and they have seized the moment to strike at the Frostmane host from the rear.', 0),
(175156, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Baugwolf\'s brow furrows deeper with every word that leaves your lips.>$B$BA cult born of Gnomeregan\'s radiation? A maddened troll god?$B$BI shall send agents north to verify these claims. After all, as you have admitted, your information comes from a troll spirit. And everyone knows what those devils are like:$B$BLying, twisted, and full of mischief.', 0),
(175158, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Another chapter in the bloody ledger between our kin.$B$BOne less grudge to settle.$B$BThose barbarians are better off dead. Their very existence, breathing our air, marking our snow with their abominable feet, is an insult to the beauty of the Makers\' legacy.', 0),
(175159, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'She is dead; I know it. The Light whispered it to me.$B$BYou have the gratitude of the Church, and though I cannot speak for them, I suspect you also have the gratitude of the troll spirits you saved from the witch\'s hunger.', 0),
(175161, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The dwarf gives the cuts of meat you\'ve brought a resounding slap.>$B$BNow this is the good stuff! Well done. You fell a bit short of a mountain, but it\'s more than enough to get us through the day.$B$BTake your share, kid!', 0),
(175174, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Candle in hand, purple skin, chattering teeth... You\'re here for the Brazier of Unity, aren\'t you?$B$B<Enril shakes his head slowly, as if to himself.>$B$BI cannot help you, I\'m afraid.', 0),
(175176, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You\'ve finished them off.$B$BI can tell because it feels a bit less bitter out here, don\'t you think?$B$BThis peak has never bared its earth from beneath the snow, and it never will; I am used to that. But those damned elementals... they actually managed to make me shiver!', 0),
(175177, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'An offering of wood.$B$BMy hunger was growing impatient. Yet, I do not recognize your face.$B$BTell me... those frost demons haven\'t harmed my Keeper, have they?', 0),
(175181, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Despite our differences, we are all one in the Light: farmers, miners, or champions like Holdor. Even kings and Thanes.$B$BMay the Light guide your steps, my child.', 0),
(175198, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'My uncle...? There was no need to send anyone. I am performing my duty as expected.$B$BThe situation at the gate is under control... though the word "control" is starting to feel generous.$B$BIn any case, thank you for taking the trouble to lend a hand in all this.$B$BI hope my uncle and the House of Nobles deign to act soon.', 0),
(175201, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Curse or no curse, they will want to hear your report in Graysky. Better than nothing, I suppose.$B$BI will stay here; you know, in case things take a turn.', 0),
(175202, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Sir Draewyn listens to your account with infinite patience.>$B$BA mine collapse. Trapped miners. Miners who, in their desperation, resort to cannibalism. And some time later, they suffer a curse that turns them into gnolls, as punishment.$B$B<For a moment, your fear Sir Draewyn might burst into laughter.>$B$BWell, stranger things have been seen in Scadeald. I will have this matter investigated further.$B$BUntil then, you have Graysky\'s gratitude.', 0),
(175203, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Alanna, poor child.$B$B<The three women, maiden, matron, and crone, speak at once, in perfect synchrony.>$B$BWe knew it would come to this, and yet we did not intervene. We never do. But Scadeald is not as we are; no. And that poor, poor man was seduced far too easily.$B$BYou are here about the cursed existence he forced upon his beloved, are you not?', 0),
(175204, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'The curse is lifting. No rain falls forever.$B$B<The Three Sisters speak in perfect synchrony. Their eyes gleam with recognition.>$B$BCarden\'s sin has been atoned for, and the balance of the Great Cycle restored.$B$BAlanna was fortunate that you found her, and no other.', 0),
(175205, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'I received a missive from Father Elroth. He cannot explain how, but it seems the threat in the crypts has been resolved.$B$BTime to pass a broom through the place, I suppose.$B$B<Sir Draewyn lifts his gaze from the documents spread across the table and gives you an approving nod.>$B$BI don\'t know what you did, but it worked.$B$BYou have Graysky\'s gratitude.', 0),
(175207, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The man\'s attire marks him as a foreigner. The dark, muted colors, as well as the bearing of his horse, identify him as a citizen of the province of Scadeald.>$B$B<His thick brows lend his gaze a certain severity.>$B$BYes?', 0),
(175208, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The mountaineer captain of Kharanos listens to you with a sharply measured dose of patience.>$B$BGramgir sends you, then. Must’ve been quite the trek from Graysky to here.', 0),
(175323, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'There it is!$B$B<Aisling takes the heart delicately, but without the faintest trace of squeamishness. Judging by the skill of her hands, you could almost think she is used to handling this and other organs.>$B$BWith this, and a little alchemy, I shall brew myself a drink that will do wonders for this poor, dry throat of mine.', 0),
(175324, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Do you have the teeth?$B$B<Aisling cups her hands to receive one tooth after another; some still bloody.>$B$BAnd they are...$B$BBear teeth, yes.$B$B<She seems relieved.>$B$BGood. Take this, for your trouble.', 0),
(175325, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Do you... feel it?$B$BEven dead, this crow\'s eye still holds a sliver of magic.$B$B<The black pupil returns a distorted reflection of Aisling\'s face, and yours.>$B$BLike a false mirror. As if someone stood on the other side... Watching.$B$BMay I give you a word of advice?$B$BWere I you, I would keep out of the crows\' sight.', 0),
(175326, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You certainly have guts. And in the right place, too.$B$BHere you are, as promised.', 0),
(175327, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'You found her!$B$B<Ulira inspects the half-digested scrap of flesh, slick with bile and stomach acid.>$B$BIt is her. It is her, beyond doubt.$B$BYou do not know how grateful I am.$B$BOh, Petunia, my poor Petunia. Did I not tell you not to leave the coop at night?', 0),
(175328, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<As he listens to your account of what happened in Cresthairn, the chaplain\'s grey brows lend his gaze a skeptical cast.>$B$BLuminous... dead?$B$BSuch a thing is simply impossible. Ealdir magic must have confused your senses. Elroth, for all his training, remains profoundly... Rural.$B$BSuperstitious.$B$BThere is nothing to investigate. Some Ealdir witch worked necromancy in those crypts. Nothing more.', 0),
(175332, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Yes, this is exactly what I was looking for.$B$B<Edren takes one of the trinkets from the pile and raises it to eye level.>$B$BI am no expert, but I have read a mountain of books on the Frostmane trolls and the ruin of their kingdom at the hands of the dwarves.$B$BI will try to discover what sort of magic these trinkets contain, and how we might counter it.', 0),
(175333, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Well. You do not realize the value of what you have brought me.$B$BIf I am not mistaken, this is a depiction of the Frostmane Loa of Death. Totez, or Totzec, or something of the sort. I would not know how to pronounce it.$B$BHere. It is all I have on me at the moment.', 0),
(175334, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'May the gods of the for— I mean, the Light bless you!$B$BThis is more than enough. And the cut is not half bad, by the way.$B$BIf you ever find yourself idle... I could find you work in the kitchen of the Plucked Rook.', 0),
(175339, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Morik feels over the pelt, searching for imperfections. He does not stop, nor say a word, until he has convinced himself that you have indeed brought him a flawless pelt.>$B$BThis will do. Now I can only hope my clumsy hands are able to sew a coat worthy of my wife.$B$BHere, as promised:', 0),
(175342, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Danael runs his hands over the black fur. He nearly rolls his eyes in delight. Then, in a high, pompous voice, he adds:>$B$BLook at the texture. Marvelous. Why, it could even serve for a coat! A cloak, perhaps. A rug, most likely. A blanket, even!$B$BHere, for your trouble...', NULL),
(175344, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Undead... touched by the Light.$B$BWhat kind of...? How is it even possible that...?$B$B<For a moment, terror paralyzes Abigail completely. A terror both physical and theological.>$B$BYou have rendered a noble service here, $r. Go. I will remain behind to ensure that none of these abominations escapes the wrath of my blade.', 0),
(175345, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'That silence... I had almost forgotten what it sounded like, with all that screeching.$B$BHere. For your trouble.', 0),
(175346, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Kildar listens to your report with a deepening frown. Whatever Senator Redstone may have thought of the matter, the captain does not appear so quick to dismiss it.>$B$BDark Iron cultists, Shadow, Fire... aye, that is no tavern rumor to be laughed off.$B$BYou did well bringing this to me. I will see that Ada Gumbad receives word, and that our agents along the border know what to watch for.$B$BIf there is trouble stirring south of Andraste, I would rather hear it coming before it reaches our door.', 0),
(175347, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Hm... What do we have here?$B$B<Beldred inspects the ore beneath the shadow of her very long nose.>$B$BThe magic emanating from these stones feels familiar.$B$BGive me everything you found, and from now on, keep away from this ore.$B$BAssuming you wish to preserve what little sanity you display by asking me for help...$B$B<A shrill laugh escapes her.>', 0),
(175348, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<Sir Draewyn listens carefully to your report on Dun Kazad\'s situation and the troubles they face.>$B$B<Then, he smiles.>$B$BYou have done good work with our neighbors, the dwarves.$B$BAs soon as we are able, I will see about sending reinforcements from Graysky.', 0),
(175349, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 'Is that...?$B$B<Ulira takes the vial with the same ceremony with which a priest might receive a sacred relic before mass.>$B$BI was going mad. I could barely sit still! All day pacing from the house to outside, and back inside again.$B$BThough I paid for it in advance at the time, please accept this. For your trouble.', 0),
(175350, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, '<The cow\'s eyes fix upon you like stakes. Something swims behind those black pupils, awakening in you a primal urge to flee.>$B$B<A voice forces its way into your thoughts. The cow\'s eyes are two wells of deep blackness.>$B$B“You have done well. Now go. And speak to no one of me.”', NULL);
INSERT INTO `quest_request_items` (`ID`, `EmoteOnComplete`, `EmoteOnIncomplete`, `CompletionText`, `VerifiedBuild`) VALUES
(175001, 0, 0, 'Dealing with these people is tedious and wearying...', 0),
(175002, 0, 0, 'Welcome to the Darengar Family Estate.', 0),
(175003, 0, 0, 'Arathon\'s room is upstairs.', 0),
(175004, 0, 0, '<Shattered crates with the Alliance seal.>', 0),
(175005, 0, 0, '<The Captain has been watching you closely since you arrived at the estate.>', 0),
(175018, 0, 0, 'Tell me you managed to recover some of my wares!', 0),
(175020, 0, 0, 'As long as there are murlocs in the lake, it doesn\'t matter how many times I cast my line... have you finished off those pests yet?', 0),
(175021, 0, 0, 'Aagh... please don\'t take too long. Do you have the plants already?', 0),
(175022, 0, 0, 'A task like this is a piece of cake for someone like you... isn\'t it?', 0),
(175025, 0, 0, 'Do you have the fey dust?', 0),
(175026, 0, 0, 'Did it work?', 0),
(175028, 0, 0, 'As long as the sails keep turning, everything will be fine.$B$BOh... do you need something?', 0),
(175029, 0, 0, 'The baker\'s main ingredient is flour. Do you have it yet, you sweet tooth?', 0),
(175030, 0, 0, 'It\'s important that the goods you bring me are fresh.', 0),
(175031, 0, 0, 'Patience... the main ingredient for anything in this life is always patience.', 0),
(175032, 0, 0, 'Any luck? My livelihood depends on that medallion...', 0),
(175038, 0, 0, 'Please tell me you have his head.', 0),
(175039, 0, 0, 'Have you found the key?', 0),
(175046, 0, 0, 'Those trout can resist even the finest fisherman... have you caught them?', 0),
(175063, 0, 0, 'Word from Galdaern Village, then? Speak.', 0),
(175064, 0, 0, 'Do you have the boar meat?', 0),
(175065, 0, 0, 'Are you done? Did you bring the feathers?', 0),
(175067, 0, 0, 'Dorothy...?', NULL),
(175076, 0, 0, 'If one goes out looking for wool, they\'d best make sure they don\'t come back shorn.', NULL),
(175088, 0, 0, 'What is that you\'re carrying? Is it for me?', NULL),
(175095, 0, 0, 'Do you have the herbs? The venom... it\'s beginning to spread.', 0),
(175096, 0, 0, 'The spirits of the trees are crying out. Have you brought them peace?', 0),
(175117, 0, 0, '<Deirdre seems far too busy with her own business to spare you a glance.>', 0),
(175118, 0, 0, 'Listen, listen.', 0),
(175119, 0, 0, 'Have you smashed those masks to bits yet, or did you lose your nerve at the sight of them?', 0),
(175120, 0, 0, 'Well?', 0),
(175124, 0, 0, 'Did you do it?', 0),
(175125, 0, 0, 'Where is that blasted book?', 0),
(175177, 0, 0, '<A crackling silhouette, wreathed in tongues of fire and curtains of black smoke.>', 0),
(175198, 0, 0, 'I do not recommend entering Scadeald at this time.', 0),
(175201, 0, 0, '<The captain appears concerned.>', 0),
(175325, 0, 0, 'The crows... If you had not noticed before, I am sure you have now.$B$BScadeald is full of them.', 0),
(175329, 0, 0, 'Can I help you with something?', 0),
(175330, 0, 0, 'Jornan Thalor, at your service. I am the bailiff of this village.$B$BCan I help you with something?', 0),
(175331, 0, 0, 'Have you brought something for me?', 0),
(175340, 0, 0, 'Back already?$B<It seems Durak has been drinking in your absence.>', 0),
(175342, 0, 0, 'I shall forgive your lack of decorum, and the absence of a bow on your part, provided you have brought me the warg pelts.', NULL),
(175349, 0, 0, 'Can I help you with something?$B$B<An ugly rash covers part of the woman\'s face, her neck, and the backs of her hands. She has scratched so much that her skin is raw.>', 0);
INSERT INTO `quest_greeting` (`ID`, `type`, `GreetEmoteType`, `GreetEmoteDelay`, `Greeting`, `VerifiedBuild`) VALUES
(164038, 0, 0, 0, 'Welcome to Cresthairn.$B$B<The bailiff gives you a long look, as though weighing you.>', 0),
(164442, 0, 0, 0, 'When word reached us that the Frostmanes had overrun the Kingsway, the first question we asked ourselves in Dun Kazad was: \'Why? What has changed?\'$B$BUntil recently, the trolls weren\'t so much a threat as they were a nuisance. A seasonal cold, if you will. They had never dared to be so bold.$B$BI have discovered the reason why.$B$BThe Horde has struck a bargain with them. They are supplying them, and they are calling the shots.', 0);
